  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=2x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=2x,$ we first replace $y$ with $2x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{(2x)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{8{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{6} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-\\sqrt{6}$ and $x=\\sqrt{6}.$",
